Docker Compose is a tool for defining and running multi-container applications. You use a single YAML file to configure your application’s 
services, networks, and volumes.

Key Benefits
One-command startup: Launch your entire stack with docker compose up.
Environment isolation: Create distinct environments (dev, test, staging) on a single host.
Preserved data: Volumes are automatically reused when containers are restarted.
Service communication: Services can talk to each other using their service names as hostnames.

Basic docker-compose.yml Structure

services:
  web:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
  database:
    image: postgres:15
    volumes:
      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

volumes:
  db_data:

Essential Commands 🚀
Start: docker compose up -d (runs in background)
Stop/Remove: docker compose down (stops containers and removes networks)
View Logs: docker compose logs -f
Check Status: docker compose ps
Rebuild: docker compose build (useful after changing a Dockerfile)

Compose vs. Standard Docker CLI
Standard: You must manually link networks and volumes for every docker run.
Compose: All relationships (links, volumes, ports) are defined in code and version-controlled.

